STAGED PATCH 0003 (kernel round) of 5: apply after 0001.
Target: Linux 6.12.116, mm/slab_common.c. Line anchors
are the manifest's numbers of record, checked against
the pristine tree; context lines are schematic.
Re-base on the real tree before applying.

Subject: iscandar: cache setup at 1.5 in kernel form;
krealloc by composition

Creation registers the cache in the mass registry
before the cache is returned to its creator: an
unregistered cache must never reach 1.1, where an
unknown cache after ready is a finding (0002). A
registration the core refuses (the cap) fails the
creation, loudly: the cap is the verdict, and a kernel
that cannot create a cache it was configured to
create has met a build figure that was drawn too
small, which is a finding about the build, not a cue
to serve stock.

The deliberate difference from the bridge staging:
registration there waited for the layer and for the
raise, and prefix-born caches were registered in a
pass at the raise. Here there is no raise and no
second party: the registry is mass memory beside the
doors and its lock is a static mutex, so registration
runs at creation in every state, prefix included, and
no registration pass exists. The prefix note still
counts the call while the prefix lasts; it is the
accounting, not the act, that is state-dependent.

Destruction deregisters first, unconditionally, for
the same reason. If grants are still live, the core
logs the verdict (0001) and the stock teardown
proceeds, since a cache being destroyed cannot be
undestroyed. The disagreement is on the record in the
log, not papered over.

Center 1.6, krealloc_noprof, takes no hunk in this
patch or any other: it composes an allocation, a copy,
and a free, and 1.1 and 1.4 are patched, so it
follows by composition. Its coverage is verified by
the manifest, not assumed silently.

